1 Introduction
In this pilot study we report on our experience of offering a Compiler Design course using an equity grading pedagogy. The course was designed to give students every opportunity to demonstrate mastery of a core set of Compiler Design skills and concepts. The final programming assignment was a compiler from a subset of C to X86-64 assembly language. The course was designed for students who had completed a Data Structures course and a Discrete Math course and it met for 50 minutes, three times a week for 13 weeks. There were three types of assessments - programming assignments covering 9 skills (Table 1), take home exams covering 12 skills (Table 2 ), and participation counting as 1 skill. The take home exams followed a Mastery Learning model. They were given every two weeks and generally assigned on a Thursday and due the following Monday morning before class. The exams had one question for each major skill/concept that had been introduced so far in the class and they were graded on a pass/fail basis. Students needed to submit both a written answer to the problem and a short video explaining their answer. Students who didn’t get a pass for a skill question, could take the corresponding question for that skill on the next exam. There were 6 exams in total and all exams skills had been introduced by the fourth exam. The programming assignments were given every two weeks with a flexible deadline a week or two in the future. Each assignment was associated with one programming skill and they were graded using a Specifications grading model in a binary fashion. They were required to submit their code and two short videos: one showing them running the program on test cases of their choosing and demonstrating it was correct, and another where they explain how their code worked and why it was correct. If they didn’t get mastery for the code or the videos, they were given feedback about what they needed to change and they were able to revise and resubmit their code and new videos, multiple times until the last day of class. Students were asked to answer 2-5 questions during each class. Collectively these in-class questions counted as one participation skill. The percentage of questions they answered with a good faith effort gave the numerical value for the participation skill. The course grade depended only on the number of take home exam, programming assignments, and participation skills they mastered by the end of the class. The take home exams had a hard deadline (the following Monday before class). The programming assignments and the participation questions had flexible deadlines, and students were responsible for deciding when they were ready to submit their answers to those questions up until the last day of class, which was a single hard deadline.
