1.1 Research Questions
We were interested in the following research questions about the equity grading pedagogy we used:
• What impact does participation mode (sync/async) have on student performance? • How many attempts does it take for students to demonstrate mastery on the exam skills and how does that correlate to skill mastery at the end of the semester? • How long does it take students to complete their programming assignments when deadlines are flexible and how is that correlated to their programming performance? • What are the students perception of equity grading?
2 Related Work
The work in this paper is part of the Grading for Equity movement within STEM education which endeavors to use pedagogies designed to help all students attain mastery of the fundamental skills in the course [12]. Some of these pedagogies include Mastery Learning [13], HyFlex course design [34], Specification Grading [22], and Flexible Deadlines [21]. Equity Grading is an approach to pedagogy proposed by Joe Feldman based on three principles:
• accurate: grades should accurately measure the student’s progress on the learning objectives for the course, and nothing else • equitable: grades should be bias-resistant, so they don’t reinforce existing inequity in the classroom • motivational: grades should be motivational
The pedagogies we used in this course were selected to help meet these three goals.
2.1 Mastery Learning
Mastery Learning has been introduced in computer science courses, most commonly in CS1 [13]. Typically, this involves giving students multiple attempts to correctly demonstrate mastery. One motivation for introducing any strategy related to Mastery Learning is to provide equity to students with diverse skill levels, especially students with less previous experience. Computer science introductory courses often have a high dropout rate and the achievement graph often shows a bimodal distribution [33]. Researchers have suggested that Computer Science courses are susceptible to the Learning Edge Momentum phenomenon [26], which describes how concepts introduced later in the course are tightly coupled with concepts introduced earlier and the result of not mastering the earlier concepts will result in students not fully understanding the later concepts. This could partly explain the bimodal distribution often seen in introductory computer science classes. Mastery Learning usually requires students to demonstrate mastery in each unit before continuing on to the next unit and there is evidence that most students who complete such a course are in favor of this approach [11]. Mastery Learning might eliminate that bimodality, as students are given multiple attempts or different questions to show mastery of the skills and concepts before they proceed to the next ones [13]. There is evidence in the literature that pedagogies based on Mastery Learning positively impact learning outcomes. One study [18] reported on an experiment that involved a two course introductory programming sequence in which the first course was an optional Python programming course, and the second was a Java course. They found that the optional course did not have any significant positive impact on students’ performance in the Java course until they introduced Mastery Learning, in which case it had a strong positive impact on student performance on the Java midterm exam. In that study, students reported that mastery learning helped them understand the material before moving on to a new concept and they felt it had a positive influence on their learning. However, not all studies of Mastery Learning in Computer Science have demonstrated that it has positive impacts on learning outcomes. Treviño, et.al. [28] explored the use of mastery learning where students were asked to work on the problems at their own pace with an information technology tool that gave immediate feedback. Their study did not show any improvement in learning outcomes, but the students liked this approach. Ott, McCane, and Meek [23] reported on their experience of shifting a course to mastery learning with a change in assessment strategies over 6 years. The paper suggests that mastery learning resulted in more procrastination across all students but this was most noticeable in the underachieving students. Furthermore mastery learning affected
