the completion rate negatively for underachieving students and resulted in more inactive periods. Other studies [16, 18, 31] indicated that the mastery learning approach requires more time and effort from the instructor to develop the assessment tasks. Additionally, they observed that students tend to procrastinate when there is no deadline and the students complained of the lack of structure in these courses that resulted from employing such a strategy.
2.2 Specification Grading
One challenge with Mastery Learning is that it may require more effort from the instructor to grade the additional assessments and/or revisions [13]. Specifications Grading [22] outlines an approach to course design aimed at streamlining the grading process without losing academic rigor by sectioning the course into skills and modules, as well as removing the requirement that students not be exposed to new material until they have mastered earlier topics. Specs grading has continued to be of interest to CS education over the last decade, particularly with the growing focus on equity and inclusion in CS [19, 25]. Related and overlapping approaches that have been explored in CS classrooms include binary grading, standards-based grading, rubric-based grading and ungrading [27]. Positive results with regard to the goals of streamlining class administration and decreasing the number of hours spent grading have come from investigations into binary grading and standards based grading in particular [3, 10, 29, 30].
2.3 Flexible Deadlines
Another aspect of Grading for Equity is to give the students more time to master the material by not having a hard deadline and by not harshly penalizing late submission of tasks to accommodate students’ differences. Boucher [7] stated that hard deadlines could increase the inequality for students and lead to stress, cheating and dropping out. While others indicated that students would eventually learn if given the time to learn the material and flexible deadlines can do just that [8]. Many studies reported on offering computer science courses (mostly CS1) using a variation of flexible deadlines such as introducing grace periods [14], late days [1], extended deadlines with increasing penalties [32], extended deadlines with fixed penalty [4, 35], and no deadline or penalty till the end of the class [17] or combination of above [2, 9, 15]. These studies generally reported positive feedback from the students after experiencing this added flexibility, as it reduced their stress, aided them in managing their time better, and improved their learning and understanding. However, some expressed concerns related to procrastination with the no deadline variant. With regard to this policy’s impact on performance, Patton [24] offered a remote programming class for 400 students without penalizing late submission of assignments, but rather giving a flexible time to solve the assignments and guiding students on how to plan their work for future submission. The results showed that with this approach students usually did the work required, finished the course, and advanced in their academic careers. Other studies of flexible deadlines also showed that students with this approach did better than students with fixed deadlines [21], and showed that this approach increased the completion rate of assignments and of the courses [24].
2.4 HyFlex Course Design
The HyFlex course design [34] is a model that combines three modes in the same course: (1) face-to-face classroom interactions, (2) synchronous virtual meetings through video conferencing tools such as Zoom or MS.Teams, and (3) traditional asynchronous online learning. In this type of course, students can choose the model that best meets their learning goals and time and they can switch between these models during the course as they like. This model is popular because of its flexibility, as the students can learn anywhere and at any time. Some previous studies using a HyFlex approach reported students’ appreciation of such models [5]. With regards to student performance, at least one study has reported no difference in students’ outcomes using this approach compared to the traditional approach [20]. Book et al., offered a CS1 course with Matlab to 412 students with a flavor of the flipped and Hyflex model in which students watch and solve questions online before attending problem-solving sessions either physically or online, though they were required to attend other physical lab session each week. Their result showed that almost 65% of the students chose online attendance and this percentage was higher in women compared to men. Also, they reported that the choice of attendance was not affected by students’ homes’ distance to campus. The study also reported that students’ choices of attendance did not impact their exam grades, but students who attended the session physically or online synchronously did better overall [6].
3 Methods
All student work in the class was collected and graded using an app developed for this style of teaching called the Mastery Learning App (MLA). We used the data collected by the MLA for the analysis in this paper. The class had 83 students, but only 25 agreed to allow us to use their data for this research project. We asked for informed consent via an email after the grades had been posted. 26 responded and 1 refused. The course contained both graduate students and undergraduates. The main goal of the programming component of the course was to guide the students in developing a compiler from a subset of C to X86_64 assembly language. The graduates were required to complete an additional programming assignment to extend the compiler in way of their own choosing (e.g. to a subset of Java). For this paper we are only focusing on the skills required for all students and so we removed the data from the graduate only assignment. The students were given a survey at the end of the course asking for their ideas about the pros and cons of the the grading for equity pedagogies used in the class. All of the data from the MLA was analyzed using a Jupyter notebook. We also incorporated data on student interaction with the echo360 server that provided and recorded access to the Zoom screen recordings. The data was analyzed using standard Python tools such as numpy, scipy, and matplotlib.
