7 Discussion
One of the distinguishing features of this work is that it combines several of the best known equity learning pedagogies and shows that they can work together successfully in an upper level Computer Science course. HyFlex Teaching Similarly to [6] we have found the participation mode (synchronous versus asynchronous) does not have a statistically significant impact on student (exam or programming) performance Most students (87%) felt we should continue to allow flexible attendance in future classes. Flexible Deadlines Most students in our study (96%) felt that we should keep Flexible Deadlines in future classes. Our results are similar to what was reported in [4, 32]. Some students were concerned that the lack of hard deadlines would lead them to procrastinate
and make the last few weeks very stressful. These attitudes were also described in [4, 15, 35]. To our minds, this expressed concern is an argument in favor of teaching and assessing effective time management skills since students are expressing their concern about their own poor time management abilities. We suspect that procrastination is a major reason that several students didn’t master most of the programming skills and that if were to teach Time Management skills and require students to report on their use as part of the programming assignments, that most students would be able to master most of the programming skills.
Mastery Learning and Specification Grading Just as with other studies (e.g. [18]) we found that students were able to reach high levels of mastery on challenging material provided they were given enough opportunities to demonstrate that mastery. Challenge Levels Mastery Learning allows us to compute a more nuanced estimation of how challenging the course was for individual students. The impact of the challenge level on their performance decreased dramatically when multiple opportunities to demonstrate mastery were offered. We haven’t seen this measure explored in previous research but it is key to understanding the benefits of Equity Grading. Limitations This was a pilot study involving just one course with 83 students and only 25 students agreed to share their course data. The 25 students’ average exam skill mastery was 11.5/12 compared to the class mean of 10.6/12; their average programming mastery was 7.0/9 compared to 6.4/9 for the entire class; so there was some self-selection bias toward higher achieving students. Also, we did not keep track of grading time; the course required fewer graders than typical classes of that size, but the last few weeks required substantially more grading.
8 Conclusions
We explored an equity grading pedagogy that strives to give the students every opportunity to learn using multiple makeup exam questions, flexible deadlines, and flexible attendance modes. The results show that the majority of students did in fact master most of the learning objectives for the course. Moreover, the level of mastery did not depend on whether they participated synchronously or asynchronously. Many students noted in open response questions that they felt that equity grading reduced stress, improved and deepened their learning, and almost all of the students felt that these teaching practices should be continued in future courses. The data we collected allowed us to measure the academic challenge levels of the exams and programming assignments in a more nuanced way than in a traditional course. One implication of this work is that students with high levels of academic challenge who might do poorly in courses with traditional assessment mechanisms could learn to master almost all of the learning objectives when given the opportunity that a grading for equity course provides.
