1 Introduction
Digital accessibility is the degree to which a digital product or service can be used by a wide range of users, regardless of their physical, mental, or cognitive capacities. This quality attribute is mostly intended to remove barriers that may prevent users with disabilities from fully understanding and operating interactive systems. However, good accessibility also implies good usability for any user [14]. Developing accessible products and services may not be a trivial task since it requires the integration of specific requirements throughout the whole development process, and it depends on the commitment of many stakeholders and executive support [24]. Many standards and guidelines have been proposed to define requirements that would help designers and developers make digital content accessible for a wide range of users with disabilities, such as the WCAG [29]. Unfortunately, despite the efforts and its relevance, studies have consistently reported the general lack of accessibility in most web and mobile applications [2]. Accordingly, researchers have conducted investigations with people involved in software development to find out the reason why accessibility tends not to be incorporated in software products and services [8, 17, 21, 24]. Most studies reached similar results, which indicate many barriers to adopting accessibility in the development process: lack of awareness and knowledge, lack of specific requirements, lack of time (functional requirements are the priority), focus on a different user profile, and lack of executive support. All studies agree that the lack of knowledge and the lack of accessibility practice are major barriers to the development of accessible products. In fact, according to the 2023 Teach Access report [1], a mere 2% of organizations report ease in hiring candidates with accessibility skills. This shortage of accessibility expertise among software professionals is largely attributed to the inadequate incorporation of accessibility topics within Computer Science (CS) curricula [6, 22, 23]. Although accessibility has been integrated into CS by several researchers in various ways [7, 13, 15, 16, 26, 28, 30], there is a notable gap in teaching the practical aspects of accessibility through projects and exploring how LLMs can assist students in learning this niche skill. Given the importance of accessibility in software development and its typical omission from standard educational programs, we have integrated a hands-on approach into a Software Engineering Course to increase students’ accessibility awareness, knowledge, and practical skills. More specifically, we recruited students of software engineering courses who have developed websites as part of their regular assignments and proposed an experience in which
