they had to: i) use accessibility checkers tools to evaluate their website; and ii) explore how LLMs can assist in fixing the accessibility violations they detected. Organizations frequently utilize accessibility evaluation tools to assess their web applications. These tools serve as a valuable complement to user accessibility testing and, in some cases, as the sole method to identify accessibility violations. We selected ChatGPT as our LLM for this experiment due to its free availability, demonstrated capability in addressing accessibility issues [20], and widespread use as an aid in software engineering tasks [9]. Additionally, ChatGPT has shown promise in improving software engineering education by providing personalized feedback and guidance to students on specific tasks [11]. After completing the assignment, we applied a post-survey to the participants, and our results demonstrate that practical assignments are highly effective in teaching both theoretical concepts and practical skills on accessibility. ChatGPT interactions help students delve deeper into their understanding of accessibility. By engaging in hands-on tasks such as developing websites, using accessibility checker tools, and fixing accessibility errors, students were able to directly apply theoretical knowledge in a practical context. This approach not only reinforced their learning but also allowed them to see the immediate impact of their work, implying that the integration of practical assignments and AI-driven interactions might be a suitable strategy to improve students’ knowledge and skills in web accessibility. This paper is organized as follows. Section 2 presents our research procedures to achieve our goals. Section 3 outlines the results, while Section 4 discusses the results. Finally, Section 5 presents some concluding remarks and future directions.
2 Study Design
The main purpose of this work is to present a teaching strategy that aims to increase the accessibility awareness, knowledge, and practical skills of students by exposing them to hands-on practices in which they can leverage LLM’s capabilities to fix accessible issues in web applications. In this section, we outline the procedures we adopted to conduct this experiment and evaluate its impact on the participants. Our study unfolded in three main steps: recruitment, assignments, and post-survey.
2.1 Recruitment
Our approach aims to enhance students’ awareness, knowledge, and practical skills in accessible development. To achieve this, we utilized a convenience sampling strategy by recruiting undergraduate and graduate students enrolled in software engineering courses at the universities where the researchers are based. In these courses, students spend the first three months developing websites of their choice, incorporating various web elements (e.g., images, buttons, interactive features). These websites vary in application domain and complexity, primarily focusing on e-commerce platforms and front-ends for AI-based services, such as recommendation systems. As a result, all participants had already developed a website that could be improved with greater accessibility features. A total of 215 students agreed to participate in the study.
2.2 Accessibility Improvement Assignment
The accessibility improvement assignment we devised is a core component of our strategy to increase participants’ awareness,
knowledge, and practical skills in this topic. Our strategy is to expose participants to practical situations in which they can identify and fix the accessibility violations of their own web code, which will allow them to grasp some knowledge of the specific accessibility requirements they should have incorporated into their website. Following that, we presented each step of the assignment, which the participants had one week to complete. Step 1. Participants were asked to evaluate the website they developed in their software engineering course by employing two accessibility checker tools: WAVE1 and Achecker2. The selection of these tools was guided by their frequent use in accessibility research [4, 5, 31, 32]. While numerous automated accessibility checker tools exist, AChecker and WAVE were specifically chosen due to their strong adherence to the widely recognized WCAG 2.0 standard [3]. Step 2. Participants were instructed to analyze the accessibility violations their website presents based on the reports provided by the tools. Following, they had to prompt ChatGPT to fix/repair the accessibility issues identified by the web accessibility evaluation tools using the code they developed and the violation descriptions provided by the tools. We selected ChatGPT over other large language models (LLMs) for the following reasons: i) ChatGPT is designed to learn from user interactions, allowing it to improve its responses over time. In the context of coding, this means ChatGPT can refine its generated code based on the feedback from developers, learning from corrections and suggestions to produce better code outputs in future interactions; ii) ChatGPT has shown outstanding performance in coding tasks compared to other LLMs in HumanEvalPlus3 benchmark [18]; iii) ChatGPT is available in a free version4, which is suitable for our participants to conduct scalable experiments; and iv) ChatGPT has been wildly adopted and evaluated in research and many applications [10, 19, 25, 27, 33]. Step 3. Participants were instructed to evaluate the websites using the two accessibility checker tools again to check the results, hence evaluating ChatGPT’s capabilities to fix problems in the code the students generated.
2.3 Post-survey
We devised a post-survey to collect information from participants after they completed the activities proposed earlier. The intent of this post-survey was to: i) collect demographic data and their general knowledge of software development; ii) understand how the participants addressed the accessibility violations they found; and iii) identify the impact of the activity on their accessibility knowledge and awareness.
2.4 Data Analysis
Our survey is mostly composed of closed questions; hence, most of our analysis will be based on basic descriptive statistics. For the few open questions we proposed, we analyzed the data using open coding qualitative analysis technique to identify recurrent categories and themes mentioned by participants.
