3 Study Results
This section presents the results of this study. First, we show information on the participant’s profile and their knowledge concerning accessibility. Next, we present the effect of this educational experiment on participant’s awareness and knowledge regarding accessibility.
3.1 Participant’s profile
In total, 215 students agreed to participate in our study. The participants are predominantly pursuing either a bachelor’s degree (50%) or a master’s degree (49%). There is a very small representation of PhD students (1%). This indicates that the majority of participants are at the undergraduate or early graduate level. The distribution between bachelor’s and master’s students is fairly balanced, with nearly equal representation from both groups. This balance can be beneficial as it provides perspectives from students at different stages of their higher education journey. All participants have some experience in software development, and most of them have developed software professionally. Table 1 shows the level of experience in software development of the participants in different dimensions: general programming (first row), web development (second row), and developing software professionally (third row).
