3.2.2 Participants’ Perceptions of Automated Accessibility Testing Tools Participants reported their experience using accessibility checker tools to find accessibility violations on their website. A significant majority of participants found using accessibility checker tools to be either “very easy” (43.7%) or “easy” (34%). Overall, this is an indication that accessibility checker tools are generally perceived as easy to use by most participants, with a combined 77.7% finding them either “very easy” or “easy” to use. 19.1% of participants rated the difficulty level as “neutral”. Only a small percentage of participants (3.3%) found the tools "challenging" to use. A significant portion of participants (44.2%) found the descriptions of accessibility violations to be very accurate and helpful, while the group (38.6%) found the descriptions to be somewhat accurate and helpful. 16.7% of participants had a neutral opinion regarding the descriptions, and only a very small percentage (0.5%) found the descriptions to be inaccurate or unhelpful. Overall, participants’ opinions show a positive reception to both the usability and the usefulness of accessibility checker tools among participants. A majority found the tools easy to use and the descriptions of violations accurate and helpful. The presence of a small neutral group and an even smaller group finding the tools challenging or the descriptions unhelpful highlights potential areas for enhancement in the assignment or tool choices. Nevertheless, the great majority of participants seemed to be able to understand which violations they had to fix using the support of LLMs.
3.2.3 Participants’ Preferred Automated Accessibility Tool As an educational exercise, we asked participants to compare the two checker tools they used. Based on the student’s feedback, WAVE emerged as a more useful accessibility evaluation tool than AChecker. Many users highlighted WAVE’s intuitive and userfriendly interface, which visually indicates accessibility issues directly on the web page. This visual feedback makes it easier for users, especially web developers and designers, to quickly identify and address problems. WAVE’s ability to provide clear, contextual information and real-time visual annotations enhances the overall user experience and efficiency in fixing accessibility errors. While
AChecker also offers valuable insights and detailed reports, its interface and feedback mechanisms were found to be less immediately accessible and engaging than those provided by WAVE. Consequently, WAVE was preferred for its simplicity, comprehensive evaluations, and ease of use, particularly for those with varying levels of technical expertise. Here is a quotation from a student that shows they indeed delve into the task of comparing both tools: "Both WAVE and AChecker are valuable accessibility evaluation tools, each with its own strengths. WAVE provides a clear visualization of accessibility issues directly on the web page, making it easy to identify problems at a glance. On the other hand, AChecker offers more detailed reports and customization options, allowing for a deeper analysis of accessibility violations. ... For quick, visual assessments, WAVE may be more convenient, while AChecker might be preferred for in-depth evaluations and customizations."
3.3 Experiences of using ChatGPT for accessibility remediation
After identifying the accessibility violations reported by the accessibility checker tools (Wave and AChecker), participants had to prompt ChatGPT to fix those issues, which means that the LLM would generate a code that is supposed to be free of the accessible problems previously discovered. We did not instruct participants on how they should write a prompt to ask ChatGPT to fix the accessibility issues. Hence, they could write the prompts they found to be more suitable for each situation. The way participants interacted with LLMs is a topic of interest in this context because it may imply the level of analysis of the violations to generate effective prompts, which is an important process of learning accessibility concepts. Therefore, we asked participants to provide us with their strategy to interact with ChatGPT in order to have the accessibility violations fixed. The most adopted strategy was to “Copy and paste the violated code fragment and add a textual description of how to fix it” (37.7%). By including both the violated code and a proposed solution, participants are guiding the model toward the desired outcome. This approach likely results in more accurate and effective fixes, as it combines problem identification with a suggested resolution. Additionally, this shows that the participants made active efforts to find solutions, an important aspect of the learning process. The second most adopted strategy was to “copy and paste the violated code fragment and add accessibility descriptions” (36.3%). A significant portion of participants chose to include both the problematic code and a description of the accessibility issue. This approach provides the model with more information, likely increasing the chances of generating a useful fix. As for the learning process, it indicates that participants had to understand the particular issues related to each violation so they could prompt the LLM to get a proper response. A proportion of participants chose to input “pairs of violated and fixed code fragments and asked the model to do the same thing for a given violated code” (12.1%). This strategy involves providing examples of both incorrect and corrected code and asking the model to replicate the correction process. Although less common, this method can be effective as it demonstrates the correction pattern, making it easier for the model to understand and apply similar fixes. The adoption of this strategy reflects an understanding of
