the model’s learning capabilities through examples and the proactivity of this group of participants in finding similar solutions to the violations they found, which is an important aspect of learning. Few participants (10.7%) adopted the strategy of just “copying and pasting the violated code fragment” as the input for ChatGPT. This strategy involves participants simply copying and pasting the problematic code without any additional context. The relatively low adoption of this strategy suggests that participants may have realized that providing more context or information yields better results when using ChatGPT. Even though participants can learn the whole process of fixing the accessibility issues using the code generated by the LLM, this strategy has less potential to impact their accessibility awareness and knowledge level. Using LLMs to generate or fix code is an interactive process in which prompts are specialized until the users reach their goals. The distribution of iterations needed to fix accessibility bugs shows a range of experiences among participants. While some were able to resolve issues quickly, many required multiple iterations, emphasizing the value of an iterative approach to debugging. This data also suggests that the LLM provides effective initial guidance but may need further refinement to enhance efficiency for all users. The need for multiple iterations by a significant portion of participants indicates areas where the LLM’s responses could be improved to reduce the number of iterations needed for successful bug fixes. About 20% of participants were able to fix the accessibility bugs with just one iteration, indicating that these participants either had a good understanding of the issue or the initial guidance from the LLM was highly effective. The majority of participants required more than one iteration to fix the bugs, with 34% using two iterations and 23.7% using three. This suggests that while the initial suggestions were helpful, additional iterations were necessary to refine and fully address the accessibility issues. A smaller proportion of participants required more iterations, with 5.6% needing four iterations and 16.7% engaging in five or more iterations. This indicates that for some participants, fixing the accessibility bugs was a more complex or challenging task, requiring multiple rounds of feedback and adjustments. Interacting with ChatGPT to fix accessibility violations was generally not time-consuming. The largest group of participants (31.2%) took one to two hours, 19.1% needed two to three hours, and 29.8% spent more than three hours. Notably, 18.1% managed to fix the issues in less than one hour. After completing the tasks, 60.5% resolved most violations, while 29.3% resolved all violations using ChatGPT. A smaller portion (8.4%) struggled with some issues, and 1.9% couldn’t resolve any. Overall, 87% found ChatGPT helpful in fixing accessibility violations.
3.4 Impact on Accessibility Awareness
One of the main goals of this study was to increase accessibility awareness and knowledge of participants by giving them the opportunity to engage in activities in which they had to evaluate a website, gather the accessibility violations, and interact with an LLM to fix the accessibility bugs. Here are the perceived results of this experience according to the participants of this study. Nearly half of the participants (47%) reported that the assignment significantly increased their understanding of accessibility. A considerable proportion of participants (43.7%) noted that the assignment somewhat
increased their understanding of accessibility. This suggests that while the assignment did have a positive impact on their knowledge, the effect was less pronounced compared to those who experienced a significant increase. However, the overall positive response indicates that the assignment was beneficial for most participants. Only a small percentage of participants (9.3%) reported a neutral impact, indicating no noticeable change in their understanding of accessibility. This group may have already possessed a solid foundation in accessibility or found the assignment less effective in enhancing their knowledge. A substantial portion of participants felt confident about building accessible websites in the future after completing the tasks and acquiring knowledge on accessibility. In total, 24.7% of the participants reported feeling very confident, and 54.4% feeling confident. This indicates that the majority of participants (79.1%) felt positive about their ability to develop accessible software, suggesting that the tasks were effective in boosting their confidence. 19.1% of participants reported feeling "neutral" about their confidence levels, while a small percentage of participants (1.9%) felt not very confident after completing the assignment.
3.5 Impact on Accessibility Attitude
Beyond evaluating the awareness and knowledge gained through this study, we queried participants about their inclination to integrate practices such as conducting accessibility checks into their future web development projects. A significant majority of participants expressed a positive inclination towards integrating accessibility practices into their future web development projects. Specifically, 44.7% reported being “very likely” and 44.2% reported being “likely” to do so. This combined 88.9% indicates a strong commitment and readiness among participants to incorporate accessibility checks, highlighting the perceived importance and value placed on accessibility in their development processes. A smaller percentage (10.2%) indicated a neutral stance regarding their likelihood to integrate accessibility checks. This group may require additional encouragement or information to fully commit to adopting these practices, suggesting an opportunity for further education or clarification on the benefits of accessibility in web development. Only a minimal percentage (0.9%) of participants expressed being “unlikely” to integrate accessibility checks into their future projects. We also asked participants whether the experiment inspired them to make immediate changes in their existing projects to improve their accessibility. A notable percentage of participants (23.7%) reported making several changes immediately after the experiment. This indicates that a significant subgroup was motivated and able to apply the knowledge or skills they acquired in the experiment to promptly enhance accessibility in their projects. A larger portion of participants (43.7%) made a few changes immediately. This suggests that while not everyone implemented extensive changes right away, a substantial number did take immediate action to improve accessibility. A considerable percentage (26%) indicated that they had not yet made changes but planned to do so later. This group shows intention and readiness to address accessibility issues in their projects in the near future, reflecting ongoing engagement and commitment to improving accessibility. A small minority (6.5%) stated that they do not plan to make changes. While this percentage is relatively
