4 Discussion
The results of our study show that engaging students with practical assignments can be effective in teaching theoretical concepts and practical skills on accessibility and that the nature of ChatGPT interactions can help students delve deeper into the topics they need to grasp. For instance, to fix the accessibility bugs, participants primarily relied on strategies that provided context and guidance to ChatGPT, with the most popular approaches involving not just identifying the problem but also describing or demonstrating the solution. This suggests a general awareness among participants that detailed input improves the quality of the model’s output. The distribution also highlights a preference for methods that help the model understand the issue more clearly and produce more accurate fixes. This is an indication that participants had to understand the problem at hand before formulating an input prompt for the LLM, allowing them to further understand accessibility requirements and interaction. When it comes to the impact of our approach in accessibility education, our results indicate that the assignment to find and fix accessibility bugs was generally effective in raising awareness and increasing knowledge about accessibility among participants. With 47% experiencing a significant increase in understanding and 43.7% reporting a moderate increase, a combined 90.7% of participants benefited from the assignment. This highlights the assignment’s overall success in educating participants about accessibility and improving their skills in this area. The small neutral group (9.3%) suggests that there is still room for refining the assignment to ensure it is helpful to all participants. In addition, the majority of participants gained confidence in their ability to find and fix accessibility bugs after completing the tasks, with 79.1% feeling either “very confident” or “confident”. In practice, our results reflect a highly positive response among participants regarding their inclination to incorporate accessibility checks into future web development projects. With 88.9% expressing a likelihood (either very likely or likely), there is strong evidence of a proactive approach towards integrating accessibility considerations. This is an indication of the effectiveness of the study in promoting awareness and fostering positive attitudes toward accessibility among participants. Furthermore, the study had a positive
impact of the experiment on participants’ willingness and ability to improve accessibility in their projects. Considering the overall experience of participants with respect to the accessibility assignment as a learning opportunity, our results indicate a predominantly positive response among participants regarding their satisfaction with the assignment as a learning experience. With 88.4% reporting either “very satisfied” or “satisfied”, there is strong evidence that the assignment effectively contributed to participants’ learning and understanding of accessibility in web development.
4.1 Key Learnings and Takeaways for Instructors:
Hands-On Assignments Enhance Accessibility Learning: Engaging students with practical assignments, such as finding and fixing accessibility bugs, effectively teaches both theoretical concepts and practical skills. This hands-on approach significantly boosts students’ understanding and awareness of accessibility issues in web development. Effective Use of AI Tools: AI tools like ChatGPT proved effective in assisting students with resolving accessibility issues. Students who provided detailed context and guidance to ChatGPT were more successful in fixing bugs, highlighting the importance of clear and thorough input. This indicates that students not only relied on AI tools but also deepened their understanding of the problems they were solving. With the industry already embracing AI tools as companions to software engineers [12], instructors can encourage students to use AI tools for accessibility bug remediation.
Boosting Confidence and Proactive Behavior: The practical experience gained from these assignments increased students’ confidence in their ability to identify and fix accessibility issues, with a majority feeling confident post-assignment. Additionally, the study fostered a proactive approach towards incorporating accessibility checks in future projects, demonstrating the assignment’s success in instilling a lasting commitment to accessibility among participants.
5 Conclusion
Our study results demonstrate that practical assignments are highly effective in teaching both theoretical concepts and practical skills, with ChatGPT interactions helping students delve deeper into their understanding of accessibility. Participants primarily used detailed, context-rich strategies to prompt ChatGPT to fix accessibility bugs, indicating their awareness of how input quality affects output. With 89.8% resolving all or most accessibility issues on their websites, ChatGPT proved effective in aiding participants, who were also able to formulate adequate prompts. The assignment significantly boosted accessibility awareness and knowledge for 90.7% of participants, highlighting the effectiveness of hands-on learning. Confidence in fixing accessibility bugs increased for 79.1% of participants, although there is still room to enhance training for the remaining 20.9%. The study also fostered a proactive approach to future accessibility checks, with 88.9% likely to incorporate them into their projects. Immediate or planned changes to improve accessibility were reported by 93.4% of participants, showing strong commitment post-experiment. Lastly, participant satisfaction with the assignment was overwhelmingly positive, with 88.4% finding it effective in enhancing their understanding of accessibility in web development.
