1 Introduction
Digital accessibility is the practice of ensuring that websites, soft
ware applications, digital tools, and technologies are usable by
individuals with disabilities. The 2024 WebAIM report reveals that
95.9% of the top 1 million websites globally do not achieve full
accessibility [6]. Major software companies and governments are
advocating for the development of accessible software [1, 4, 24, 34].
However, the 2023 Teach Access report [5] indicates that only 2%
of organizations find it easy to hire candidates with accessibility
skills. This scarcity of accessibility expertise among software profes
sionals is primarily due to the insufficient inclusion of accessibility
topics in Computer Science (CS) curricula [9, 26, 28].
Accessibility education is often delivered through elective courses
by instructors specializing in human-computer interaction (HCI)
[9, 32]. However, these courses are not mandatory for all computer
science students, resulting in many students missing out on critical
accessibility training. Elglaly et al. [13] support a wider integra
tion of accessibility topics throughout the CS curriculum, beyond
the confines of HCI courses. They suggest a comprehensive set of
knowledge units, including specific topics and learning objectives,
to form a dedicated Accessibility Knowledge Area (KA). To bet
ter equip students with essential accessibility skills, we integrated
accessibility training into a software engineering (SE) course. We
dedicated one sprint specifically to accessibility, recognizing the
importance of hands-on experience in mastering this critical area.
Although this is not the only approach, as accessibility has been
incorporated into several other CS courses (as discussed in Section
2), the SE course provided a practical setting. We made this decision
for several reasons:
• Practical Application: By incorporating accessibility into
a practical course like software engineering, students can
directly apply what they learn in real-world scenarios, en
hancing their understanding and retention of accessibility
principles.
• Comprehensive Skill Development: Focusing on acces
sibility within software engineering courses ensures that
students view it as a fundamental aspect of software devel
opment rather than an optional or peripheral concern.
• Industry Relevance: Given the increasing emphasis on
accessibility in the software industry, this approach better
prepares students for their professional careers’ expectations
and demands.
